From 3d237f0843855395f9a0df4236e658a99f1f7333 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Struan Bartlett <struan.bartlett@NewsNow.co.uk>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:50:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vhost_user: Optionally adapt the polling window to the
 guest

Add --queue-poll=none|fixed|adaptive, the way to poll the request queue
after it runs dry, with --queue-poll-us=N now the window: fixed's, or
adaptive's largest (default 50 microseconds).  "none" is upstream's
behaviour, and the default; "fixed" polls for N microseconds each time,
as --queue-poll-us=N alone also does.

"adaptive" polls for a window that starts at zero and adapts to how
closely the guest's requests follow one another, as QEMU's adaptive
polling of its event loops does: when the next request arrives after
polling has given up, but within N microseconds of the queue running
dry, the window grows (to 4 microseconds, then doubling, up to N); when
it takes longer than N, the window halves (to zero below 4); when
polling catches it, the window stays as it is.  So a guest issuing
requests in quick succession is polled for, while an idle guest, or one
whose requests are far apart, costs no polling.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
